nsistent 25th percentile growth since birth (constitutional feature)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Cardiac-related systemic syndrome: congenital heart disease may impact growth but does not explain respiratory tract involvement", "Recurrent infectious pneumonias without structural lung component: contradicts MRI findings", "Primary immunodeficiency: less likely given chronic respiratory pattern suggests structural rather than immune deficiency", "Environmental exposure syndrome: possible but low-tier without specific evidence"], "alarm_features": ["History of tricuspid stenosis", "Persistent growth in 25th percentile since birth", "Airway dilation from MRI (objective evidence of underlying pathology)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Genetic/Metabolic Disorder with Multisystem Involvement", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.5618497109826588, "posterior": 0.4291763724229044, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Presentation consistent with primary ciliary dyskinesia (a multisystem genetic condition), which explains both sinus and cardiac findings better than isolated structural airway disease.", "Primary ciliary dyskinesia (B1) better explains the chronic multisystem issues (sinuses, airways, heart) than isolated structural airway disease (B5), but structural findings (B5) remain relevant due to imaging confirmation."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of neurological signs or family history mildly contradicts multisystem genetic/metabolic disorders but supports a focused condition."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Presence of other congenital anomalies", "Family history of similar symptoms or early death", "Developmental delays or organ dysfunction"], "requestable_discriminators": ["CFTR genetic testing", "Whole exome sequencing", "Sweat chloride measurement (if applicable)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Chronic Inflammatory Airway Disease", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.07092445155234155, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Chronic airway dilation supports a structural airway disorder with genetic implications but doesn't clarify its relationship to the cardiac findings."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Congenital Anomaly Syndrome (Cardiac + Respiratory Axis)", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.03782637416124884, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Primary ciliary dyskinesia (B1) better explains the chronic multisystem issues (sinuses, airways, heart) than isolated structural airway disease (B5), but structural findings (B5) remain relevant due to imaging confirmation."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Primary Immune Deficiency with Complicated Course", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.008865556444042693, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Structural Airway Abnormality with Unspecified Genetic Aetiology", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.20809248554913293, "posterior": 0.05957653930396691, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["No documented visceral anomalies on MRI reduces strength of the comprehensive anomaly syndrome hypothesis, but cardiac/respiratory axis remains consistent.", "Chronic airway dilation on MRI and lifelong respiratory symptoms moderately support B5 as a structural airway abnormality but fail to resolve the overlap with tricuspid stenosis etiology."], "evidence_against": ["Presentation consistent with primary ciliary dyskinesia (a multisystem genetic condition), which explains both sinus and cardiac findings better than isolated structural airway disease.", "Primary ciliary dyskinesia (B1) better explains the chronic multisystem issues (sinuses, airways, heart) than isolated structural airway disease (B5), but structural findings (B5) remain relevant due to imaging confirmation."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B5.1", "B5.2", "B5.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Consistency of airway dilation across previous imaging", "Neurological signs (dysphagia, facial weakness)", "Family history of structural disorders"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Airway function tests (PFT/PFT)", "Genetic panel for airway disorders", "Bronchial brushings for mucociliary analysis"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "pathophysiology"}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia (PCD) with Cardiac Anomalies", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.24331258909015052, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Autosomal Recessive Ciliary Dyskinesia (Non-PCD Variants)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.1520703681813441, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Genetic Immunometabolic Syndrome with Cardiopulmonary Involvement", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.033793415151409795, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Normal growth and no signs of systemic organ failure reduce the suspicion for immunometabolic syndromes. This contradicts the typical presentation of B1.3 despite its multisystem classification."]}, "B5.1": {"id": "B5.1", "label": "Ciliary Dyskinesia with Cardiopulmonary Involvement", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.039895003998192125, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B5", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of situs anomalies on MRI suggests against congenital structural airway pathology. The lack of expected visceral malposition in ciliary syndromes weakens B5.1."]}, "B5.2": {"id": "B5.2", "label": "Developmental Bronchial Malformations with Isolated Cardiac Defect", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.017021868372561977, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B5", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The strong pathogenic link between ciliary function and both cardiac/airway development argues against separate developmental anomalies. This supports genetic syndromes over isolated structural explanations."]}, "B5.3": {"id": "B5.3", "label": "Syndromic Airway-Cardiac Structural Disorder (e.g., Loeys-Dietz variant)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0026596669332128084, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B5", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The lack of connective tissue features directly contradicts syndromic disorders like Loeys-Dietz. Structural airway-cardiac syndromes typically require extra-cardiac findings in their diagnostic criteria."]}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the chronic sinus disease and tricuspid stenosis (both present from birth) suggest a broader developmental anomaly consistent with primary ciliary dyskinesia (B1.1) rather than isolated airway dilation (B5.1), given the strong association between ciliary dysfunction and cardiac/lung laterality defects?", "result_summary": "The coexistence of cardiac and respiratory developmental anomalies is strongly pathognomonic for ciliary dyskinesia syndromes. Tricuspid stenosis and chronic sinus issues without situs anomalies weaken the structural airway hypothesis (B5.1).", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "strong_for", "B1.1": "strong_for", "B5": "moderate_against", "B5.1": "moderate_against"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the documented findings (normal neurological development, no family history of ciliary syndromes, and growth percentiles consistent since birth) inconsistent with autosomal recessive ciliary variants (B1.2), which often lack these classic PCD features but still demonstrate cardiac/respiratory involvement?", "result_summary": "Absence of classic PCD features (e.g., situs anomalies) is neutral for autosomal recessive ciliary variants but does not exclude them. These findings are less consistent with comprehensive developmental anomalies.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "moderate_for", "B1.2": "weak_for"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the stable growth percentiles and absence of documented neurological dysfunction inconsistent with genetic immunometabolic syndromes (B1.3), which typically present with progressive organ dysfunction and atypical growth patterns?", "result_summary": "Normal growth and no signs of systemic organ failure reduce the suspicion for immunometabolic syndromes. This contradicts the typical presentation of B1.3 despite its multisystem classification.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_for", "B1.3": "strong_against"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of documented visceral situs anomalies on MRI contradict the congenital structural airway pathology hypothesis (B5.1), given such anomalies are expected with ciliary dyskinesia syndromes and would be easily detectable via MRI?", "result_summar