ac findings in their diagnostic criteria.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B5": "moderate_against", "B5.3": "strong_against"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 1, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the coexistence of cardiac and respiratory developmental anomalies with normal organ development (no visceral situs anomalies on MRI) support the autosomal recessive ciliary variants (B1.2) rather than primary ciliary dyskinesia (B1.1), given the lack of classic PCD features but persistence of multisystem involvement?", "result_summary": "The coexistence of congenital cardiac and respiratory anomalies without visceral situs anomalies provides weak support for autosomal recessive ciliary dyskinesia variants (B1.2) and weakens the likelihood of primary ciliary dyskinesia with cardiac anomalies (B1.1), as situs anomalies are a core diagnostic feature of classical PCD."}, {"timestep": 5, "bundle_id": 5, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 1, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the coexistence of lifelong respiratory symptoms (since infancy) and cardiac developmental anomaly with no documented systemic organ dysfunction contradict the prototypical findings for genetic immunometabolic syndromes (B1.3), which typically manifest with both cardiopulmonary and progressive multisystem involvement?", "result_summary": "The absence of documented systemic organ dysfunction or atypical growth patterns in this patient, despite presenting with cardiac and respiratory anomalies, moderately supports autosomal recessive ciliary dyskinesia variants (B1.2) while strongly argues against genetic immunometabolic multisystem syndromes (B1.3). This reinforces the broader genetic/metabolic disorder hypothesis (B1) but weakens its immunometabolic subcategory."}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.4291763724229044, "B2": 0.07092445155234155, "B3": 0.03782